# Advising statement

This is placeholder copy for Debasmita’s advising statement. Replace it with your real mentoring philosophy when ready. The goal of this page is to give prospective students a clear sense of how PEARL works day to day—expectations, communication, and how we grow together as researchers.

## What I care about as an advisor

I want students to leave PEARL as independent researchers who can identify important problems, design careful studies or systems, and communicate their work clearly. At the same time, research is a team sport: we celebrate each other’s wins, share hard problems early, and treat failed experiments as useful information rather than personal setbacks.

I try to balance structure with autonomy. Early on, we define concrete milestones and check in often. As you gain confidence, you take more ownership of the research direction while I remain available as a sounding board, co-author, and advocate.

## Mentoring style

- **Regular meetings.** Students typically meet with me one-on-one each week, with additional project meetings as needed. Come prepared with updates, blockers, and questions.
- **Written plans.** We keep lightweight notes on goals for the semester so progress is visible and adjustable.
- **Feedback.** I give candid, timely feedback on writing, talks, code reviews, and experimental design. I also expect you to push back when something does not make sense.
- **Collaboration.** Most projects involve working with peers, undergraduates, or external collaborators. Mentoring junior students is part of growing as a researcher in PEARL.

## Expectations for students

I look for students who are curious, reliable, and excited to build and evaluate systems that interact with people. You do not need to arrive knowing every skill the lab uses, but you should be eager to learn, document your work, and ask for help before you are stuck for too long.

A typical week includes reading papers, implementing or iterating on experiments, analyzing results, and writing. Workloads vary by role (Ph.D., master’s, undergraduate) and by project phase, but consistency matters more than occasional late-night sprints.

## Lab culture

PEARL is a new lab, which means early members help shape infrastructure, practices, and culture. We aim for an environment that is ambitious and kind: high standards for the work, respect for people’s time and wellbeing, and room to discuss ideas that are still half-formed.

If something is not working—technically, personally, or in the advising relationship—I want to hear about it early so we can course-correct together.

## Funding, authorship, and professional growth

We discuss authorship expectations at the start of projects and revisit them as contributions evolve. I support students in applying for fellowships, presenting at conferences, and exploring internships or collaborations when those opportunities strengthen their research.

Funding models differ by role and year; we talk openly about what support is available and what timelines look like.

## Is PEARL a good fit?

If you are excited about robots that work with and around people, enjoy both algorithmic and empirical work, and want to help build a lab from the ground up, we may be a strong match. If you prefer highly independent work with infrequent feedback, or projects that never leave simulation, another group might suit you better—and that is okay.

For open positions and how to apply, see the [Join the lab](/join) page.
